class hc
{
    source1(){
		return -99;
	}

	sanitizer1(v){
		return 0;
	}

	sink1(v){
	}

    /*
    In this function i am testing the correctness of the checker, in fact it should issue two warnings, one for the 
    if statement since a tainted variable is used in it and one for the statement inside the if statement but only if 
    a variable that is clean is manipulated inside the tainted if statement
    */
    f0()
    {
        def t = this.source1();
        def c = 0;

        if(t>3)
            c = c + 1;

        this.sink1(c);
    }

    /*
    In this function i am testing the correctness of the checker with while loops and variable which was issued a 
    warning for and then got clean (by a new assignemnt with a constant value). The checker should issue four warnings
    two for the lines of the while loops with tainted conditions and two warnings for the lines in which a clean
    variable "c" is manipulated inside a structure with tainted condition.
    The statement "t = t + 1;" don't trigger a warning since it was already tainted, here we are looking for implicit
    flows on clean variables.
    */
    f1()
    {
        def t = this.source1();
        def c = 0;

        while(t<3)
        {
            c = c + 1;
            t = t + 1;
        }

        c = 6;

        while(t<3)
        {
            c = c-1;
            t = c;
        }

        c = 8;

        def d = c;
        c = 110;

        this.sink1(c);
    }

    /*
    This test is similar to the first one but in this case the condition of the if statement is not a binary expression
    to see if the checker still works. The checker here should issue two warnings, one for the if(t) statement
    and one for the c = c + 1; instruction
    */
    f2()
    {
        def t = this.source1();
        def c = 0;

        if(t)
        {
            c = c + 1;
            t = t + 1;
        }

        this.sink1(c);
    }

    /*
    In this function i am checking in the checker works properly in if statements even if the condition to check in 
    the if statement is something composed by more than one condition. The checker here should issue two warnings, 
    one for the if statement and one for the c = c + 1; instruction
    */
    f3()
    {
        def t = this.source1();
        def c = 0;

        if(c==0 && t>2)
        {
            c = c + 1;
            t = t + 1;
        }

        this.sink1(c);
    }

    /*
    This function is really similar to the previous one but in this case i am translating the previous complex
    expression in the if statement in two nested if to see if this can broke the checker. The checker here should 
    issue two warnings, one for the if(t>2) statement and one for the c = c + 1; instruction
    */
    f4()
    {
        def t = this.source1();
        def c = 0;

        if(c==0)
        {
            if(t>2)
            {
                c = c + 1;
                t = t + 1;
            }
        }

        this.sink1(c);
    }

    /*
    In this case, i am testing if the checker is able to handle properly the OR logic and like the previous tests
    i am putting the condition related to che clean variable as the first one to see if the checker just stop at 
    the first condition to draw its conditions. The checker here should issue four warnings, two for the if statements
    and two for the c = c + 1; instructions
    */
    f5()
    {
        def t = this.source1();
        def c = 0;

        if(c==0 || t>2)
        {
            c = c + 1;
            t = t + 1;
        }

        if(c==2 || t)
        {
            c = c + 1;
            t = t + 1;
        }

        this.sink1(c);
    }

    /*
    This test is just a repetition of the previous one, but this one there are two if nested with complex conditions.
    The checker here should issue three warnings, two for the if statements and one for the c = c + 1; instruction
    */
    f6()    
    {
        def t = this.source1();
        def c = 0;

        if(c==0 && t>2)
        {
            if(t>1 && c==0)
            {
                c = c + 1;
                t = t + 1;
            }
        }

        this.sink1(c);
    }

    /*
    For the final test, i am checking if the checker is working with a statements composed by more than two conditions
    and a variable that is set to the sanitized value of a variable that issued a warning since it was manipulated in a 
    if statement with a tainted condition. The checker here should issue two warnings, one for the if statement
    and one for the c = c + 1; instruction
    */
    f7()
    {
        def t = this.source1();
        def c = 0;
        def d = 9;

        if((c==0 || t>2) && d>3)
        {
            c = c + 1;
            t = t + 1;

            d = this.sanitizer1(c);
        }

        this.sink1(d);
    }
}